  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nMinor coastal flooding will remain a concern along the Middle Texas \nCoast through at least the middle of the week. P-ETSS guidance \ncontinues to indicate elevated water levels coinciding with higher \nastronomical tides, potentially resulting in minor inundation of \nbeaches and beach roads, particularly around times of high tide. \n\nAbove normal temperatures will persist across South Texas through \nmuch of the upcoming work week as midlevel ridging remains in place \nahead of a cold front and cooler temperatures by next weekend. \nMeanwhile, a substantial increase in atmospheric moisture and \npassing low-level disturbances will support periodic rain chances.\n\nPWATs this morning around 1.25\" at CRP are expected to increase \nrapidly over the next 24 hours, peaking around 2.3 - 2.4\". Despite \nthe anomalously high moisture content, limited large-scale forcing \nshould keep rainfall coverage relatively low. However, weak low-\nlevel disturbances and mesoscale boundaries may provide sufficient \nlift for isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms through \nmuch of the week. The greatest rain chances are expected Monday, \nparticularly across the northern Coastal Bend and Victoria \nCrossroads, where moderate probabilities are forecast. Otherwise, \ngenerally low rain chances will persist through the remainder of the \nweek. \n\nAttention then turns to a cold front expected to approach South \nTexas late in the week. Operational guidance has trended toward \nbetter agreement regarding the front reaching the forecast area, \nalthough notable differences remain in its timing and strength. The \nGFS favors a Friday FROPA, while the Euro delays the boundary until \nSaturday. These differences introduce uncertainty regarding the \nonset and magnitude of post-frontal cooling, along with the \nstrength of the associated northerly winds. Higher-resolution \nguidance, including the NAM, should help refine these details as \nthe event draws closer. Given the remaining uncertainty, there is \nlittle reason to deviate from the NBM at this time. \n\nHigh temperatures will generally range from the lower 90s to around \n100 degrees through much of the week before falling into the lower \nto middle 80s behind the front. Overnight lows could fall into the \nupper 50s to mid 60s across inland areas, with milder conditions \nalong the immediate coast. These temperatures remain subject to \nadjustment as confidence increases in the timing and strength of the \nfront. \n\nIn addition to cooler temperatures, the frontal passage could \nproduce a period of strong northerly winds, potentially approaching \nWind Advisory criteria. Confidence in the occurrence and magnitude \nof these winds remains limited, but the potential will need to be \nmonitored in subsequent forecasts.",
